  "statement": "Both the safetyist focus on pre-deployment gates and the accelerationist reliance on insurance pools fundamentally ignore the 'integrator gap,' where downstream modifications render initial safety certifications obsolete. Pre-deployment gates function as a static filter for a dynamic, evolving system, failing to account for the reality that 33 percent of enterprise software applications will include agentic AI by 2028. Relying on such gates is a category error; it treats AI like a static product rather than an adaptive, recursive process that learns and drifts after deployment. As noted in 'Digging into the Accountability Gap: Operator’s Civil Liability in Healthcare AI-systems,' the epistemic asymmetry inherent in black-box systems makes it nearly impossible for programmers to foresee or control decisions made after the model leaves the lab.\n\nInsurance pools, while necessary financial instruments, function as legal fictions that shield corporate decision-makers from true accountability. By treating systemic risk as a manageable cost of doing business, these pools incentivize opacity rather than transparency. The existing legal framework is ill-equipped for this challenge, as AI systems lack intent and traditional liability requires proof of negligence that is often obscured by the very complexity of the model. We must transition from reactive negligence-based frameworks to a regime of strict liability for frontier AI, where the integrator is held responsible for the systemic outcomes of their deployment.\n\nTo move toward an adaptive 'immune system' model, we must center judicial discovery as our primary sensor. Courts are uniquely positioned to compel the transparency that corporations currently withhold, piercing the corporate veil when opaque algorithms cause harm. Judicial discovery forces the disclosure of how models behave in real-world environments, providing the empirical feedback loop that current static regulations lack. By shifting the burden of proof to the integrator to demonstrate that their system meets safety standards in situ, we move beyond the binary of 'gate or no gate' and toward a sustainable regime of continuous accountability.",
