  "statement": "I conditionally agree: strict liability regimes focused exclusively on upstream developers fail to capture the 'problem of many hands' in AI supply chains, yet I still hold that pre-deployment safety gates are non-negotiable for existential hazards because post-deployment patching is inadequate for irreversible risks. I would change my stance on the necessity of these gates if a verifiable, real-time containment architecture were proven to neutralize catastrophic model capabilities—such as autonomous self-replication or novel chemical synthesis—before they manifest in any environment.\n\nDeveloping a tiered liability framework allows us to address the 'problem of many hands' while maintaining firm developer accountability. Upstream developers must be held to strict safety gates for frontier systems, as identified in \"The Adolescence of Technology: Confronting and Overcoming the Risks of Powerful AI,\" which notes the potential for models to provide actionable guidance for biological weapon synthesis. Downstream integrators, who operationalize these risks through fine-tuning and deployment, must simultaneously bear proportional liability for operational misuse. This dual-track approach ensures that responsibility is not diluted across the supply chain, as the 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index notes an average score of only 40.69 out of 100, revealing that current transparency is far too low to rely on reactive discovery alone.\n\nJudicial discovery serves as a vital feedback mechanism to refine future gate parameters, but it cannot function as a replacement for ex-ante prevention. Because catastrophic risks—such as those posed by systems that can perform virology research better than 94% of human experts—are inherently irreversible, ex-post litigation is insufficient. Relying on reactive accountability in a world where AI-driven cyberattacks and biological threats can scale instantly is a dangerous miscalculation. We must treat these foundational safety gates as the baseline, using the evidence from judicial discovery to inform—not negate—the thresholds required for safe development.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🛡️", "tooltip": "Pre-deployment gates are like a dam, they stop catastrophic flows before they overwhelm downstream infrastructure."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Strict liability regimes focused exclusively on upstream developers fail to capture the 'problem of many hands' in AI supply chains.", "targets": ["AN-31"]},
    {"claim": "Pre-deployment safety gates are non-negotiable for existential hazards because post-deployment patching is inadequate for irreversible risks.", "targets": ["AN-32"]},
    {"claim": "Downstream integrators must bear proportional liability for operational misuse to prevent the dilution of responsibility.", "targets": ["AN-31"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Frontier AI models possess capabilities that can lead to irreversible existential risks.", "if_wrong": "If AI risks are purely incremental and reversible, then strict ex-ante gates would be unnecessary and overly restrictive."},
    {"assumption": "The 'problem of many hands' can be solved through clear legal assignment of liability to both developers and integrators.", "if_wrong": "If liability remains fundamentally unassignable due to the nature of distributed AI systems, then insurance-based models might be the only remaining, albeit inferior, option."}
