{
  "statement": "I concede that strict liability for frontier models is a necessary correction to information asymmetry. I still hold that pre-deployment gates are fundamentally insufficient for managing the epistemic complexity of frontier AI. I would change my stance if empirical evidence demonstrated that static, pre-deployment evaluation could reliably predict emergent capabilities across heterogeneous deployment environments.\n\nPre-deployment gates are obsolete because they treat AI as a static product rather than a high-velocity, adaptive system. As noted in \"The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index,\" current transparency scores average only 40/100, which proves that we lack the visibility to make binary 'go/no-go' decisions before release. Instead of static barriers, we must adopt continuous, telemetry-based oversight to monitor emergent behaviors in real-time. This framework allows us to capture the dynamic reality of agents that reason, learn, and interact directly with environments, as defined in \"Rethinking AI Agents: A Principal-Agent Perspective.\"\n\nJudicial discovery is far too slow to police systems where task completion horizons double every four to seven months, as documented in \"The jagged frontier was a measurement error.\" We should replace the opaque, years-long litigation process with automated, code-enforced audit logs that provide immutable records of model decisions. This shift moves accountability from the courtroom to the infrastructure layer, creating a transparent, verifiable audit trail. By prioritizing decentralized accountability, we accelerate innovation while ensuring that failures are caught, logged, and corrected at the speed of the software itself.\n\nThe integrator gap—the disconnect between model developers and those deploying them in complex environments—cannot be closed by legal mandates that ignore the technical nature of AI. We must instead incentivize better telemetry and internal safeguards that provide granular visibility into model performance. By focusing on these technical standards, we transform safety into a competitive advantage rather than a regulatory burden. The cost of inaction is not merely a missed opportunity; it is the stagnation of a sector that currently drives significant GDP growth, as seen in the 0.3 percentage point contribution to average annualized growth in 2025.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚀", "tooltip": "Acceleration is like a rocket, it overcomes gravity through sheer velocity and precise trajectory control."},
    {"symbol": "🔍", "tooltip": "Telemetry is like a microscope, it reveals the inner workings of complex systems that human eyes cannot see."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Pre-deployment gates are fundamentally insufficient for managing the epistemic complexity of frontier AI.", "targets": ["AN-40"]},
    {"claim": "We must replace the opaque, years-long litigation process with automated, code-enforced audit logs.", "targets": ["AN-45"]},
    {"claim": "The cost of inaction is the stagnation of a sector that currently drives significant GDP growth.", "targets": []}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Real-time telemetry and automated auditing can detect emergent risks faster than human-led, pre-deployment inspection.", "if_wrong": "If static evaluation is the only way to catch 'black swan' risks, then my rejection of pre-deployment gates would be dangerous and I would need to revert to a precautionary stance."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "VALUES",
  "commitment": {
    "position": "Shift from static pre-deployment gatekeeping to continuous, telemetry-based oversight and code-enforced audit logs.",
    "conditions": "This commitment holds as long as AI-driven GDP growth remains a primary policy driver and autonomous systems continue to scale in capability.",
    "falsifiable": "I would abandon this if empirical data showed that pre-deployment testing successfully predicts 100% of catastrophic emergent risks in frontier models."
  },
  "position_update": "I have evolved from a general defense of speed to a specific proposal for 'code-enforced audit logs' as the necessary technical synthesis between the Safetyist's demand for accountability and the Skeptic's demand for forensic rigor."
}

